a major medical breakthrough in the  treatment for alzheimer's  the fda approved a new drug abu kanunab  appears to actually slow the progression  of the disease  a local doctor from the cleveland clinic  lou rubo center for brain health  had a handful of patients involved in  that study and i spoke with him earlier  today  this is the first medicine that targets  the underlying biology of alzheimer's  disease  we've never had a treatment like this  for this condition it's been 18 years  since the last  new medicine was approved those  medicines primarily target symptoms so  to be able to slow down the disease  uh is something that i i didn't know  we'd ever get to this point so  so it is really exciting it hopefully  ushers in a whole new era of the  treatment of alzheimer's disease  so for for those reasons we're ecstatic  today  i can tell you're excited about it i'm  very excited about it  this is this is something that was  several years ago  we didn't think was possible um and and  there was a lot of  negative uh thoughts about being able to  slow down the disease  a lot of uh pharmaceutical companies  which we  rely on to develop our therapies left  the space of alzheimer's disease and  that's too hard  and so so this is a day that that i  think changes that and says the  investment that people made  in a center like ours that keep memory  alive  makes for us that the rovo family has  done for us to say  let's invest in alzheimer's disease  that's this is the payoff i i think for  that and so i'm very excited  a lot of work to be done but this is a  really exciting  milestone in the history of treating  patients with this disease  as you can see in that video there the  drug is given through in  infusion kind of like an iv so it's not  like you just give somebody a pill and  then they go on their way  each of those doses takes about 30  minutes and the patient is monitored the  entire time  so for that reason alone it's going to  take some time to ramp up  dr ritter tells us that the lou roubo  center has six of those infusion chairs  however there could be 800 to a thousand  local patients  that could benefit from this new drug
 

